AN  - PMID:35708888
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35708888/
N2  - Pooling 227 results from 69 studies, a night of sleep loss lowered exercise performance by about 7.6% on average (95% CI 3.1% to 11.9%), and performance fell in every type of exercise tested. Losses were biggest after a full night without sleep or after waking early, grew by about 0.4% for each extra hour awake, and showed up consistently in afternoon or evening tests but much less in morning tests; the reported drop in strength was small (about 3%).
N1  - Systematic review with meta-analysis (multi-level) and meta-regression: 227 outcome measures from 69 publications comparing normal sleep with acute sleep loss (total deprivation, early or late sleep restriction). Sports Med 2022;52:2669-2690, PMID 35708888.. Verified 2026-10-14. Used for: recovery.sleep_loss_impairs_performance: supports
