N2  - Pooling randomised trials and cohort studies, it found that adults on unrestricted diets who cut sugars lost about 0.8 kg on average (95% CI 0.39 to 1.21 kg), and those who added sugars gained about 0.75 kg. Swapping sugars for other carbohydrates at the same calories made no difference (0.04 kg), so the effect seems to come from eating fewer calories, and trials in children showed no overall weight change.
N1  - Systematic review and meta-analyses of randomised controlled trials and prospective cohort studies in free-living adults and children (Te Morenga, Mallard, Mann; BMJ 2013;346:e7492, PMID 23321486; PubMed lists the date as 2012 Jan 15). My searches did not show the counts of 30 RCTs and 38 cohort studies.. Verified 2026-10-14. Used for: nutrition.free_sugars_and_health: supports
